#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_solve_values

#% text
# `n_solve_values(eq, x)` gives the numerical values of the solutions of `eq` for `x`, as a list `[a, b, ...]`.

#% text
# `n_solve_values(eqs, [x, y, ...])` gives one list of values per solution, `[[a1, b1, ...], ...]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It takes the same equations, domains and options as `n_solve`, and gives the values of `n_solve`'s rules in the order of the unknowns.

#% item
# The roots of a polynomial in one unknown are ordered by real part, then by imaginary part.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The five roots of a quintic:

#% cell
n_solve_values(x^5 - 2*x + 3 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [-1.4236058485523317, -0.24672925691056402 - 1.3208163474502475im, -0.24672925691056402 + 1.3208163474502475im, 0.9585321811867299 - 0.498427779031846im, 0.9585321811867299 + 0.498427779031846im]

#% text
# The real roots only:

#% cell
n_solve_values(x^5 - 2*x + 3 == 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= [-1.4236058485523317]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A system, one list per solution:

#% cell
n_solve_values([x*y == 1, x + y == 3], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[0.3819660112501051, 2.618033988749895], [2.618033988749895, 0.3819660112501051]]

#% text
# A transcendental equation over the reals, through `product_log`:

#% cell
n_solve_values(e^x - x == 7, x, reals)
#% output
#= [-6.999087285366495, 2.22154230138681]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_solve`, `solve_values`, `find_root`
